    "body": "Blue Origin is investing $554.78 million in a manufacturing complex in Hutto, Texas , that will add more than 2,000 jobs for its satellite communications business. Texas Gov. Greg Abbott announced the investment along with an $18.17 million state grant.\nWhat’s Related\nBlue Origin said the site, called Constellation Park, will span 1.3 million square feet across multiple facilities. The company expects to add manufacturing and other jobs over the next 10 years.\nThe complex will make hardware for Blue Origin’s TeraWave satellite communications service and Quartz ground communications system. It will also produce solar arrays, onboard electronics , telecommunications equipment, and other satellite components.\nThe governor’s office described the project as under construction. Neither the state nor Blue Origin disclosed an opening date or a production start date for the Hutto facilities.\nManufacturing for satellite and ground communications\nTeraWave is designed for businesses , data centers, and government customers. Blue Origin says it will offer upload and download speeds of up to 6 terabits per second through a planned system of 5,408 satellites in low and medium Earth orbit.\nThe company plans to begin deploying those satellites in the fourth quarter of 2027. That schedule applies to TeraWave’s satellite rollout; Blue Origin has not said when manufacturing in Hutto will begin.\nConstellation Park will also expand production for Quartz, which uses ground-based antennas to communicate with spacecraft. Its initial ground stations feature 3.7-meter antennas that operate on two radio frequency bands used for low Earth orbit missions.\nBlue Origin announced in August that it had installed and tested its first three Quartz ground stations in Bermuda, New Zealand and Australia. It planned to expand the system to nine sites by the end of 2026.\nState funding\nTexas has extended an $18,171,000 Texas Enterprise Fund grant to Blue Origin, along with a $10,000 Veteran Created Job Bonus. The state describes the fund as a performance-based program for business relocations and expansions competing with locations outside Texas.\nThe Hutto project will add to Blue Origin’s existing Texas workforce of more than 550 people. Its operations in the state date back to the establishment of Launch Site One in 2006.\n“Texas has long understood what it takes to lead in space exploration,” said Blue Origin CEO Dave Limp. The company said the Hutto expansion would also create opportunities for local suppliers and other businesses.\nKUT reported that Blue Origin plans to work with Hutto ISD on paid high school internships and career and technical education programs. Those plans would connect local students with training as the company builds its workforce.",
